    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "It may sometimes be desirable to mix XML configuration files, Groovy scripts, and component classes (typically "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes) to configure an "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for your tests. For example, if you use XML configuration in production, you may decide that you want to use "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes to configure specific Spring-managed components for your tests, or vice versa."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Furthermore, some third-party frameworks (such as Spring Boot) provide first-class support for loading an "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " from different types of resources simultaneously (for example, XML configuration files, Groovy scripts, and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes). The Spring Framework, historically, has not supported this for standard deployments. Consequently, most of the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "SmartContextLoader"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " implementations that the Spring Framework delivers in the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "spring-test"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " module support only one resource type for each test context. However, this does not mean that you cannot use both. One exception to the general rule is that the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "GenericGroovyXmlContextLoader"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "GenericGroovyXmlWebContextLoader"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " support both XML configuration files and Groovy scripts simultaneously. Furthermore, third-party frameworks may choose to support the declaration of both "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "locations"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "classes"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " through "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@ContextConfiguration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", and, with the standard testing support in the TestContext framework, you have the following options."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "If you want to use resource locations (for example, XML or Groovy) and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes to configure your tests, you must pick one as the entry point, and that one must include or import the other. For example, in XML or Groovy scripts, you can include "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes by using component scanning or defining them as normal Spring beans, whereas, in a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " class, you can use "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@ImportResource"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " to import XML configuration files or Groovy scripts. Note that this behavior is semantically equivalent to how you configure your application in production: In production configuration, you define either a set of XML or Groovy resource locations or a set of "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Configuration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " classes from which your production "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " is loaded, but you still have the freedom to include or import the other type of configuration."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
    "title": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "value": "Mixing XML, Groovy Scripts, and Component Classes"
      }
    ]
  }
